course: biology
lesson: 324
title: Falcons, hawks, kites, ospreys and vultures
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Birds of prey come in many shapes, each made for its own job. The peregrine falcon is a missile, the kestrel a helicopter, the sparrowhawk a hedge-hopping ambusher, the osprey a fishing hook and the vulture a flying clean-up crew.
explain: Picture a kestrel over a motorway verge: it faces the wind, flaps fast and holds its head perfectly still while it watches the grass for a vole. Then it drops.
explain: Looks can fool you. Falcons look like hawks, but DNA, the tiny instructions inside every living cell, shows they are closer cousins of parrots and songbirds. They look alike because they do the same job, hunting.
explain: Vultures are not villains. Cartoon vultures circle over a lost traveller, waiting, but real ones circle to ride warm rising air while they search for animals that are already dead, and without them dead animals rot and spread disease.
fact: In a steep dive a peregrine falcon can go over 300 kilometres an hour, the fastest of any animal.
fact: DNA studies show falcons are closer relatives of parrots and songbirds than of hawks and eagles; they look alike because they do the same job.
fact: Kestrels hover by flapping into the wind while keeping the head still, watching for voles and mice, and scientists reported they can see ultraviolet light, which may make vole wee trails glow.
fact: Sparrowhawks hunt garden birds by surprise, darting low along hedges, and the female is about twice as heavy as the male.
fact: Red kites, with forked tails, nearly died out in Britain, leaving only a few pairs in Wales; from 1989 people brought kites in from other countries, and now there are thousands.
fact: Aboriginal people and fire crews in Australia report kites and falcons picking up burning sticks and dropping them in dry grass to flush out food; scientists are still studying how often this happens.
fact: An osprey can turn one outer toe backwards, giving two toes forward and two back to grip slippery fish, and it carries the fish head forward like a torpedo.
fact: Ospreys live on every continent except Antarctica and dive feet first into water.
fact: A vulture's bald head stays cleaner when pushed inside a carcass, and its very strong stomach acid kills most germs in rotten meat.
fact: In India a medicine given to cattle poisoned the vultures that ate dead cattle, and almost all of them died; dead animals lay rotting and stray dogs and rats increased, and the medicine was then banned for cattle.
fact: The bearded vulture eats mostly bones and drops big ones onto rocks from high up to crack them.
fact: Vultures circle to ride rising warm air and search wide areas; they do not attack living people, and when one drops down, others come from far away.
exercise: Which animal is the fastest on Earth in a dive? | The peregrine falcon | The golden eagle, diving from the clouds | The cheetah on the open plain | The common swift of summer
exercise: How fast can a peregrine falcon dive? | Over 300 kilometres an hour | Only about 30 kilometres an hour | About as fast as a racing bicycle | Faster than a rocket into space
exercise: Which birds are falcons' closest relatives, according to DNA? | Parrots and songbirds | Hawks, kites and the big eagles | Owls and the big vultures | Ducks, geese and swans
exercise: Why do falcons and hawks look alike? | They do the same job, hunting, not because they are close cousins | They are the same kind of bird, just at different ages, young and old | Falcons copy hawks on purpose | They both come from eagles
exercise: How does a kestrel hover in one place? | It flaps into the wind and holds its head still | It sits on the wind like a bird sits on a branch | It hangs from an invisible thread | It floats like a balloon
exercise: What might help a kestrel find voles in the grass? | Seeing ultraviolet light, which may make vole wee trails glow | Smelling voles from high up in the clouds with its sharp nose | Hearing voles sing at night | Feeling voles through its feathers
exercise: How does a sparrowhawk catch garden birds? | By surprise, darting low along hedges | By chasing them high into the clouds | By waiting in a nest box | By singing to lure them in
exercise: In sparrowhawks, which is bigger? | The female, about twice as heavy as the male | The male, which is about twice as heavy as the female | They are always exactly the same | The babies, which shrink later
exercise: How did red kites come back in Britain? | People brought kites in from other countries from 1989 | A cartoon about a kite made people love them, so they stopped hunting them | Kites flew back from the Moon | They were always common everywhere
exercise: Why might kites in Australia drop burning sticks into dry grass? | To flush out insects, lizards and mice to eat | To scare people and their cars away from the roads | To keep their chicks warm | To build nests out of ash
exercise: How does an osprey grip a slippery fish? | It turns one toe back, so two point forward and two back | It grabs the fish with its beak and holds it tight while flying | It wraps its wings round it | It sticks to it with sticky feet
exercise: How does an osprey carry its fish? | Head forward, like a torpedo | Tail first, like a flag in the wind | Sideways, like a knight's shield | In its beak, like a dog with a stick
exercise: Why does a vulture have a bald head? | It stays cleaner when pushed inside a carcass | Its head feathers all fell out because of old age | It is cooler for flying at night | It shines to scare away lions
exercise: What happened to vultures in India? | A cattle medicine poisoned them and almost all of them died | They all flew away to Africa, where there was much more food | They grew too fat to fly | They became pets in cities
exercise: How does a bearded vulture break big bones? | It drops them onto rocks from high up | It chews them up with big, strong teeth | It boils them soft in bubbling hot springs | It stamps on them hard with its big feet
exercise: Why do vultures circle in the sky? | To ride warm rising air and search for dead animals | To wait for a living person to get tired and fall down | To scare other birds away | To dry their feathers in the sun
test: «falcon's just a small hawk, everyone knows that» What does DNA show? | Falcons are closer to parrots and songbirds than to hawks | Yes, a falcon is just a baby hawk that has not grown up yet | Falcons are a kind of owl | Falcons are closer to bats than to birds
test: In a cartoon three vultures circle over a lost man in the desert, waiting. What are real vultures doing when they circle? | Riding warm air and searching wide areas for animals already dead | Waiting for the living man to stop moving so they can attack him | Counting the people below | Dancing to call the rain
test: In The Jungle Book four vultures sit on a dead tree and chat. Count them: how many wings and how many talons on all their feet? | 8 wings and 32 talons | 4 wings and 16 talons | 8 wings and 16 talons | 8 wings and 40 talons
test: Count them: an osprey holds a fish in both feet with two toes forward on each. How many toes point back in all? | 4 | 2 | 1 | 6
test: Count them: 6 red kites fly over a field. How many forked tails? | 6 | 12 | 3 | 18
test: «ugh the hawk ate a sparrow at the feeder, that's evil» What is the best answer? | It is natural; sparrowhawks must eat small birds to live | Yes, sparrowhawks kill just for sport and leave the bodies behind | Hawks only eat seeds normally | The sparrow was asleep anyway
test: What would happen if a town's vultures all died? | Dead animals would rot longer and rats and stray dogs could increase | The town would become much cleaner, because vultures spread dirt and germs | Lions would move in to clean up | Nothing, vultures do no work
test: Why is a motorway verge a good place for a kestrel to hunt? | The long grass is full of voles and mice | Cars give kestrels free food every day | Kestrels like the noise | The verge is warmer than the sky
test: «a falcon diving's faster than it flying flat, why tho» What is the best answer? | Diving uses gravity, with wings folded tight | Falcons are lazy when flying flat | The air is thinner near the ground | Falcons push off clouds with their feet
test: Why might the grass beside a vole hole glow for a kestrel but look plain to you? | Kestrels are reported to see ultraviolet light that we cannot | Kestrels have eyes that glow like torches and light up the grass | The voles paint their paths | The grass glows only at night
test: A cattle medicine is banned in India and vultures slowly return. Which is true? | One medicine shows how harming one animal can cause trouble for people | Vultures returned only because people began feeding them bread every day | Vultures never cared about the medicine | The ban made cattle sick instead
exam: Identify the animal which attains the greatest velocity of any animal in a descending stoop. | The peregrine falcon, exceeding three hundred kilometres per hour | The golden eagle, exceeding six hundred kilometres per hour | The cheetah, exceeding three hundred kilometres per hour | The common swift, exceeding one thousand kilometres per hour
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the kinship of falcons. | Genetic evidence indicates that falcons are more closely related to parrots and songbirds than to hawks | Genetic evidence indicates that falcons are the juvenile form of hawks, which they come to resemble with age | Genetic evidence indicates that falcons descend from owls | No relationship between falcons and other birds can be determined
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the circling flight of vultures? | It exploits rising warm air to survey extensive areas for carrion | It is a preparation for an attack upon living persons | It is a ritual performed before rain | It is employed to dry the plumage
exam: Ascertain the principal consequence of the decline of vultures in India. | Carcasses remained unconsumed and the numbers of rats and stray dogs increased | The countryside became cleaner than before, carcasses being removed more swiftly by rain | Cattle ceased to require any medicine | Lions assumed the role of the vultures
